Q: What is Redis?

Redis (Remote Dictionary Server) is an **in-memory** data store used as:
- cache
- fast key-value DB
- pub/sub / streams broker
- session store, rate limiter, distributed locks

Extremely fast because primary working set lives in **RAM**.
Persistence to disk is **optional** (RDB snapshots / AOF).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔥 Why use Redis?

- Microsecond-level latency for simple ops
- Rich data structures (not only plain strings)
- TTL / eviction policies (perfect for cache)
- Pub/Sub and Streams
- Simple ops: GET/SET, atomic increments, sorted sets

────────────────────────────────────────
📌 Common use cases

| Use case          | Example                              |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Caching           | Cache DB query / API response        |
| Sessions          | Spring session store across instances |
| Rate limiting     | counters + TTL                       |
| Leaderboards      | Sorted sets                          |
| Distributed locks | SET NX EX / Redlock patterns         |
| Messaging         | Pub/Sub or Streams                   |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 Data types

| Type       | Typical use                |
| ---------- | -------------------------- |
| String     | tokens, counters, cache    |
| Hash       | object fields (user:1)    |
| List       | simple queues              |
| Set        | unique tags/ids            |
| Sorted Set | rankings by score          |
| Stream     | durable-ish message log    |

